# stair — the interior grand stair, 1.80 to 6.30, twenty-five risers in two flights round a half
#         landing, with a turned balustrade and a moulded handrail.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — read this before you move a single number.
#
# THE HEAD-HEIGHT CHECK UNDER A FLIGHT OF STAIRS. It is the one geometric constraint in this
# building that cannot be satisfied by eye, and it is the only one the engine's own `walkability`
# CANNOT CHECK FOR YOU. measure.cpp walks a HEIGHTFIELD: for every column it finds the topmost
# non-air voxel and asks for clear air above it. A floor under an overhang is not the topmost
# matter in its column, so it is not a surface, so it is never tested. Stand under this stair and
# `walkable` has nothing to say about you. Written up in clips/facility/requests/stair.md.
#
# So this file measures it itself, and the measurement is a binding you can run:
#
#     .\build\bin\WorldShaper.exe --clip-file clips\facility.clip --clip-metre 32 ^
#         --clip-part stair_probe_head
#
# `stair_probe_head` is the volume a person's head sweeps through every part of this stair —
# 2.10 m above every floor, every tread and every landing, between the balustrades — INTERSECTED
# with the stair itself. IT MUST REPORT AN EMPTY CLIP. Anything it reports is a place somebody
# hits their head. It measured empty at metre 32 with the numbers below, and two of those numbers
# exist for no other reason:
#
#   THIRTEEN RISERS TO THE HALF LANDING AND TWELVE ABOVE IT, and not twelve then thirteen.
#   Twelve first puts the landing at 3.96; less the thinnest slab the module allows it gives
#   1.935 m under it, and the passage under this stair fails by 165 mm. Thirteen first puts it
#   at 4.14 and the same slab gives 2.115. The split is decided by the head height and by
#   nothing else, and it is the first thing anybody "tidying" this file will reverse.
#
#   THE WAIST OF A FLIGHT IS 0.405 (0.9 M) AND NOT 0.45 (M). The upper flight's soffit leaves
#   the half landing at the same level the landing's own soffit is at; a whole module of waist
#   puts that at 3.87 and the head height at 2.07, and it fails by 30 mm. 0.9 M puts it at 3.915.
#
# The tightest clearance in the finished stair is therefore 2.115 m, under the half landing and
# under the first metre of the upper flight. FIFTEEN MILLIMETRES. Half a voxel at the contract's
# metre 32. Move the landing slab, the waist, the riser count or the floor and it goes negative,
# and no render will tell you: a soffit 65 mm too low looks exactly like a soffit 65 mm too high.
#
# Secondary loads, all of them free riders on geometry that had to exist anyway:
#
#   THE BALUSTERS. Sixty-two of the order's `ionic_baluster` to a stair, a hundred and twenty-four
#   in the building, each with a 0.063 m neck — two voxels at metre 32, the second-thinnest matter
#   in the building — and THIRTY-FIVE OF THEM ARE ON A RAKE, so no two are at the same height and
#   none shares a voxel row with its neighbour. The parapet's balusters all repeat off one plane
#   and a sampler can settle one box round the lot; these cannot, and they are placed by doubling
#   rather than by `repeat` for exactly that reason.
#
#   THE ROTATED FRAME, three times: each raking handrail is one straight moulded section built
#   about the origin and turned by atan(0.18/0.32) about z, and the upper flight's soffit is a
#   rotated half-space. Rotations are isometries so the field stays exact and keeps a box. The
#   great steps do the same thing about x; this is the only mass in the building rotated about z,
#   so between the two of them a regression in rotated bounds has nowhere to hide.
#
#   A ROOM ONLY LIGHT CAN GET INTO SIDEWAYS. The passage under the stair is 2.115 m high, 22 m2,
#   and its only daylight is the two ground-storey north windows at x = 10.80 and 13.50, whose
#   heads are at 4.50 — level with the middle of the upper flight, so everything under the half
#   landing is lit by bounce off a floor. Polished marble treads over a granite floor with a
#   bronze handrail above them, all of it in shadow.
# ================================================================================================
#
# WHERE IT IS, AND WHY IT IS THERE
#
# The north strip of the east wing, mirrored to the west. halls.clip has the middle of both wings
# (x 4.95..13.95, z +-2.925, up to 6.525) and says in writing that the strips z 2.925..6.60 are
# left for somebody else. This is that strip: 3.675 m deep, which is why the stair runs east-west
# and not north-south — a flight is 3.84 m long and would not fit across it.
#
# TWO STAIRS AND NOT ONE. The brief says "in one wing"; BRIEF.md rule 3 says symmetric about x = 0
# and does not qualify it. One stair in the east wing and none in the west is the one thing that
# would read as a mistake from the plan, so the whole part is `mirror { } axis=x` and there is a
# matching stair in each wing. Everything below is built on POSITIVE x, because mirror folds the
# coordinate to its absolute value and a shape drawn on -x simply disappears.
#
# WHAT IT KEEPS OUT OF. windows.clip punches its openings 1.80 m in from the outer wall faces:
# ground storey y 2.70..4.50, piano nobile y 6.30..8.55, at x = 10.80 and 13.50 on the north wall
# and at z = 0, +-2.70, +-5.40 on the east wall, each 1.35 wide. Anything of mine inside one of
# those is deleted by the manifest's `hollowed` difference, silently. So:
#
#   - every horizontal slab here has its TOP at 4.14 or at 6.30 and its underside above 3.90 or
#     above 5.89, which puts every one of them in the 4.50..6.30 band that has no opening in it.
#     The gallery slab can therefore run right into both walls and be bedded in them.
#   - every BALUSTRADE stands clear: none is nearer than 0.90 m to the limit of any opening.
#     The gallery's rails are at z = 4.59 and x = 11.71, the openings begin at z = 5.70 and
#     x = 14.20. Checked against windows.clip's own `windows_cuts`, not assumed.
#   - the upper flight passes the two north windows at y 5.05..6.16 — over their heads at 4.50
#     and under their piano-nobile sills at 6.30 — which is the only band it could pass them in.
#
# WHERE IT MEETS ITS NEIGHBOURS
#
#   podium.clip      the floor at 1.80. Everything that stands is founded at 1.75.
#   walls.clip       north inner face z = 6.60, east inner face x = 15.10 (block_z - D and
#                    block_x - D, both contract numbers). The half landing, the upper flight and
#                    the gallery are bedded 0.15 into them.
#   halls.clip       its block face is z = 2.925 and its top is 6.525. Nothing here comes nearer
#                    than z = 3.15, and the nearest thing of mine at that height is 3.45 m away
#                    in plan, so the 0.225 difference between its 6.525 and this stair's 6.30
#                    can never be a step anybody walks. See requests/stair.md: 6.30 is 1.80 plus
#                    twenty-five risers and is also the piano nobile's window sill, so it is the
#                    upper floor and halls is 0.225 proud of it.
#
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45. Every number below is a multiple or simple fraction of one of
# those, or of the stair's own two contract numbers, the riser 0.18 (0.4 M) and the run 0.32.
# The ones that are neither are constructions and not dimensions, and each is named where it
# occurs: the rake 0.08154932 turns (atan(0.5625)/tau), the flight ends at 11.62 and 11.94 (the
# foot plus eleven and twelve runs), and the clipping frames, which are only knives.
#
#   6.75 = 15 M   half landing west face      1.80  floor              podium_top
#   8.10 = 18 M   half landing east face      1.75  founding, 0.05 into the podium
#  11.62         upper flight head            3.915 landing soffit     4.14 - M/2
#  11.94         lower flight foot            4.14  half landing       1.80 + 13 x 0.18
#  15.10         east wall inner face         5.895 gallery soffit     6.30 - 0.9 M
#  15.25         0.15 into that wall          6.30  upper floor        1.80 + 25 x 0.18
#   3.15 = 7 M   south face of the stair      0.925 handrail crown over the surface a baluster
#                                                   stands on: 0.70 + M/2
#   4.50 = 10 M  lower flight / well
#   5.40 = 12 M  well / upper flight
#   6.60         north wall inner face
#   6.75 = 15 M  0.15 into that wall

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE LOWER FLIGHT — thirteen risers, 1.80 to 4.14, ascending WEST, on a solid spandrel
# ================================================================================================
#
# Twelve treads of 0.32 from x = 11.94 back to 8.10, the thirteenth riser being the half landing
# itself. Twelve nested boxes, each set back one run and raised one riser from the one below and
# each running west to x = 8.00 so that it is buried in the landing — the same construction the
# great steps use, and for the same reason: the whole value of a stair is that its treads are
# IDENTICAL, and a box is the only thing in this language that cannot drift.
#
# They all reach down to 1.75, which makes the flight a SOLID SPANDREL rather than a raking slab.
# That is not laziness. The wedge under a flight is between zero and 2.1 m of head height and
# there is no honest way to let anybody in there; filling it is what a stone stair on a spandrel
# actually is, it carries the west end of the half landing, and it takes that whole volume out of
# the head-height probe by making it not a place.
#
#   tread  1   x 11.94   top 1.98        tread  7   x 10.02   top 3.06
#   tread  2   x 11.62   top 2.16        tread  8   x  9.70   top 3.24
#   tread  3   x 11.30   top 2.34        tread  9   x  9.38   top 3.42
#   tread  4   x 10.98   top 2.52        tread 10   x  9.06   top 3.60
#   tread  5   x 10.66   top 2.70        tread 11   x  8.74   top 3.78
#   tread  6   x 10.34   top 2.88        tread 12   x  8.42   top 3.96
#
# The nosing round is 0.015 (M/30), the great steps' own, chosen against the voxel grid rather
# than by eye: at metre 32 it pulls a tread top down by at most a tenth of a voxel at a sample,
# which is never enough to move a tread into the voxel below and report a seven-voxel rise.

# EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS 0.015 INSIDE THE FACE IT MAKES, and that is not a typo. `round=r` on a
# box GROWS it: the flat faces move out by r and the arris is turned with radius r through the
# corner you wrote, so a box written to a tread top of 1.98 has its tread at 1.995. Harmless on
# its own — but the half landing is written the same way, and 15 mm off its soffit is 15 mm off
# the only clearance in this building that has 15 mm to give. The head-height probe caught it as
# 86 voxels of landing hanging into the passage. So the numbers are written short and the round
# puts them back, which is the idiom halls.clip uses on its transverse arch for the same reason.
#
# Riser 0.18 and run 0.32 are UNAFFECTED either way, because every box is shifted the same; what
# is affected is where the flight sits against the floor and the landing, which is everything.
let stair_a_01 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   11.925 1.965 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_02 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   11.605 2.145 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_03 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   11.285 2.325 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_04 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   10.965 2.505 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_05 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   10.645 2.685 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_06 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   10.325 2.865 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_07 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165   10.005 3.045 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_08 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165    9.685 3.225 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_09 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165    9.365 3.405 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_10 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165    9.045 3.585 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_11 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165    8.725 3.765 4.485 round=0.015
let stair_a_12 = box 8.015 1.765 3.165    8.405 3.945 4.485 round=0.015

let stair_flight_a = union {
    stair_a_01 stair_a_02 stair_a_03 stair_a_04 stair_a_05 stair_a_06
    stair_a_07 stair_a_08 stair_a_09 stair_a_10 stair_a_11 stair_a_12
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE HALF LANDING — 1.35 deep (3 M), top 4.14, soffit 3.915
# ================================================================================================
#
# x 6.75 .. 8.20 and the full width of the stair, z 3.15 .. 6.75, bedded 0.15 into the north wall.
# It runs 0.10 east of its own face at 8.10 so that the upper flight, which starts there, is
# certainly one solid with it rather than two shapes touching on a plane.
#
# 0.225 THICK, WHICH IS M/2, AND IT IS THE THICKEST THING THAT FITS. 4.14 less the brief's 2.10
# less the floor's 1.80 leaves 0.24. A whole module of slab misses by 210 mm and a third of one
# (0.15) would have been thin enough — but M/2 is a module fraction, it is what the order uses
# for a fascia, and it leaves the margin at fifteen millimetres rather than at ninety, which is
# the honest place to leave it: this is a constraint, and a constraint you have cleared by 90 mm
# is a constraint you have stopped measuring.
# THE FACE IS AT 8.10 OVER THE LOWER FLIGHT AND AT 8.20 UNDER THE UPPER ONE. The 0.10 is the
# overlap the upper flight needs so the two are one solid rather than two shapes touching on a
# plane — but carried right across it would stand 0.10 of landing on top of the lower flight's
# twelfth tread and leave that tread 0.22 deep instead of 0.32, which is a 0.18 step in the middle
# of a stair. So the lip is only where the upper flight buries it. The probe found this too.
let stair_land_slab = box 6.765 3.930 3.165   8.085 4.125 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_land_lip  = box 8.015 3.930 5.415   8.185 4.125 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_landing = union { stair_land_slab stair_land_lip }

# ================================================================================================
# THE UPPER FLIGHT — twelve risers, 4.14 to 6.30, ascending EAST, a raking slab
# ================================================================================================
#
# Eleven treads of 0.32 from x = 8.10 to 11.62, the twelfth riser being the gallery. This one is
# NOT filled underneath: the passage under it is the point, and its soffit is the surface the
# head-height probe measures against.
#
# The soffit is a plane parallel to the nosing line and 0.405 (0.9 M) below it — a rotated box,
# which is exact and keeps a bounding box where a `plane` written by hand would be neither. Its
# local frame stands on the first nosing at (8.10, 4.32) with local +x running up the rake, and
# it is turned by 0.08154932 turns about z, which is atan(0.18/0.32) / tau. A third box gives the
# flight a VERTICAL west face at x = 8.10; without it the rotated box's own end cuts the first
# riser on the slant and the stair starts with a wedge.
#
#   tread  1   x  8.42   top 4.32        tread  7   x 10.34   top 5.40
#   tread  2   x  8.74   top 4.50        tread  8   x 10.66   top 5.58
#   tread  3   x  9.06   top 4.68        tread  9   x 10.98   top 5.76
#   tread  4   x  9.38   top 4.86        tread 10   x 11.30   top 5.94
#   tread  5   x  9.70   top 5.04        tread 11   x 11.62   top 6.12
#   tread  6   x 10.02   top 5.22
#
# Tread eleven runs on to 11.67 so that it is buried in the gallery, whose face at 11.62 is the
# twelfth riser.

# THE NESTING GOES THE OTHER WAY ROUND FROM THE LOWER FLIGHT, and getting that wrong is silent.
# A nested-box stair is a stack of boxes each of which runs from its own riser AWAY IN THE
# DIRECTION OF DESCENT, so that every box below is buried in the ones above. The lower flight
# climbs west, so its boxes all end at the same eastern edge; this one climbs east, so its boxes
# all start at the same western edge. Written the lower flight's way — every box from x = 8.10 out
# to its own riser — the tallest box covers all the others and the flight comes back as a SOLID
# BLOCK 2.16 m high with a flat top. One component, no floating voxels, no error, and it measured
# 25,124 voxels, which is a plausible number for a flight of stairs. The head-height probe over
# the treads is what found it.
let stair_b_01 = box  8.115 3.215 5.415   11.655 4.305 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_02 = box  8.435 3.215 5.415   11.655 4.485 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_03 = box  8.755 3.215 5.415   11.655 4.665 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_04 = box  9.075 3.215 5.415   11.655 4.845 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_05 = box  9.395 3.215 5.415   11.655 5.025 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_06 = box  9.715 3.215 5.415   11.655 5.205 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_07 = box 10.035 3.215 5.415   11.655 5.385 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_08 = box 10.355 3.215 5.415   11.655 5.565 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_09 = box 10.675 3.215 5.415   11.655 5.745 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_10 = box 10.995 3.215 5.415   11.655 5.925 6.735 round=0.015
let stair_b_11 = box 11.315 3.215 5.415   11.655 6.105 6.735 round=0.015

let stair_b_steps = union {
    stair_b_01 stair_b_02 stair_b_03 stair_b_04 stair_b_05 stair_b_06
    stair_b_07 stair_b_08 stair_b_09 stair_b_10 stair_b_11
}

# THE SOFFIT PLANE IS PLACED BY A POINT ON IT, not by an offset below the nosing line, and the
# difference is 60 mm of head height. `rotate` turns the box about the origin, so a box whose
# bottom face sits 0.405 BELOW the local origin has that face 0.405 away PERPENDICULAR to the
# rake — which is 0.405 / cos(29.4 degrees) = 0.465 straight down. Written that way the soffit
# left the landing at 3.855 and the passage was 2.055 m high. Written this way — the box's bottom
# face ON the local origin, and the origin carried to the soffit's own corner at (8.10, 3.915) —
# the waist is 0.405 VERTICALLY at every point of the flight, because the soffit and the nosing
# line are parallel and 0.405 apart in y by construction rather than by trigonometry.
let stair_b_waist_sec = box -1.00 0.00 5.415   4.30 3.00 6.735
let stair_b_waist = translate { rotate { stair_b_waist_sec } z=0.08154932 } 8.10 3.915 0
let stair_b_face  = box 8.10 3.00 5.40   12.00 7.00 6.75

let stair_flight_b = intersection { stair_b_steps stair_b_waist stair_b_face }

# ================================================================================================
# THE GALLERY — the upper floor this stair arrives at, top 6.30, soffit 5.895
# ================================================================================================
#
# x 11.62 .. 15.25 by z 4.50 .. 6.75: 3.48 by 2.10 of walkable upper floor, bedded 0.15 into the
# east wall and 0.15 into the north wall. Its top is the piano nobile's own window sill, which is
# how you know 6.30 is right: 1.80 plus twenty-five risers of 0.18 lands on the level windows.clip
# already put its upper sills at.
#
# It is 0.405 thick, the flight's own waist, so the two soffits are one continuous plane and the
# joint between them at x = 11.62 has no step in it at all.
#
# WHEN SOMEBODY BUILDS THE REST OF THE UPPER FLOOR it butts this: the open edges are z = 4.50
# from x 11.62 to 15.10, and x = 11.62 from z 4.50 to 5.40. Both carry a balustrade now because
# a landing over a 4.5 m drop has to; take either of them out when there is a floor on the other
# side of it, and leave the one over the well.
let stair_gallery = box 11.635 5.910 4.515   15.235 6.285 6.735 round=0.015

# ================================================================================================
# THE PIERS — what carries the landing and the gallery over the passage
# ================================================================================================
#
# Square, 0.45 (M), founded at 1.75 and running up 0.015 into the slab above so nothing is left
# resting on a plane. Two under the west edge of the half landing, two under the west edge of the
# gallery, all four on the same 0.45 grid.
#
# THEY HAVE NO PLINTH AND NO CAP AND THAT IS DELIBERATE, TWICE OVER. A cap projecting 0.045 under
# a 3.915 soffit is a 45 mm ring of stone at 1.935 m over a floor people walk on — it fails the
# head height that this whole file exists to hold. And a plinth 0.225 tall beside a floor is a
# 0.225 rise in a building whose worst rise is supposed to be the steps' 0.18; measure.cpp counts
# any rise up to 0.75 m as a step somebody meant to take. So the ornament is SUNK instead: a panel
# 0.045 deep (M/10) in each face, which costs nothing in either check and gives the passage
# something to be lit by.
# FOUR CUTTERS AND NOT TWO, ONE TO A FACE, EACH 0.045 DEEP. Two cutters — one across x and one
# across z, each run past both faces — is the obvious way to sink a panel on all four sides of a
# pier and it does not sink anything: each one goes straight THROUGH, and their union takes the
# whole middle out and leaves four corner posts. It reads as a pier from a metre away, it costs no
# components and no floating voxels, and the only way to see it is to stand under the stair, which
# is where the first path-traced view of this passage was taken from.
#
# 0.09 (M/5) DEEP AND NOT 0.045, and the reason is paint rather than shadow. A rule keyed on a
# recess has to name a zone that holds the recess floor and excludes the face 45 mm outside it,
# and the rules' own `below=0.02` reaches 20 mm each way off a voxel centre that is itself up to
# 16 mm off the surface. There is no room in 45 mm; there is in 90. halls.clip found the same
# thing from the other side — at 45 mm its sunk panels did not read at all — so this is the
# building's second vote for the same number. Inset 0.09 from every arris, so the corner is whole.
let stair_pier_body = box  6.765 1.765 4.515    7.185 3.930 4.935 round=0.015
let stair_pier_pan_s = box 6.840 2.25 4.410    7.110 3.45 4.590
let stair_pier_pan_n = box 6.840 2.25 4.860    7.110 3.45 5.040
let stair_pier_pan_w = box 6.660 2.25 4.590    6.840 3.45 4.860
let stair_pier_pan_e = box 7.110 2.25 4.590    7.290 3.45 4.860
let stair_pier_one = difference {
    stair_pier_body
    union { stair_pier_pan_s stair_pier_pan_n stair_pier_pan_w stair_pier_pan_e }
}

let stair_pier_a = stair_pier_one
let stair_pier_b = translate { stair_pier_one } 0 0 1.35

let stair_tpier_body  = box 11.715 1.765 4.515   12.135 5.925 4.935 round=0.015
let stair_tpier_pan_s = box 11.790 2.25 4.410   12.060 5.40 4.590
let stair_tpier_pan_n = box 11.790 2.25 4.860   12.060 5.40 5.040
let stair_tpier_pan_w = box 11.610 2.25 4.590   11.790 5.40 4.860
let stair_tpier_pan_e = box 12.060 2.25 4.590   12.240 5.40 4.860
let stair_tpier_one = difference {
    stair_tpier_body
    union { stair_tpier_pan_s stair_tpier_pan_n stair_tpier_pan_w stair_tpier_pan_e }
}

let stair_pier_c = stair_tpier_one
let stair_pier_d = translate { stair_tpier_one } 0 0 1.80

let stair_piers = union { stair_pier_a stair_pier_b stair_pier_c stair_pier_d }

# ================================================================================================
# THE BALUSTRADE — the order's turned baluster, one to a tread
# ================================================================================================
#
# `ionic_baluster` is 0.70 tall on a plinth 0.18 square, which is exactly one run less 0.14, so
# one to a tread at 0.32 centres leaves a 0.14 gap and a 0.063 neck. The handrail over it is
# 0.225 tall, so the rail crown stands 0.925 above the surface a baluster stands on.
#
# 0.925 IS NOT A COMFORT DECISION. measure.cpp reports the worst rise between neighbouring columns
# and treats anything over 0.75 m as a wall rather than as a bad step. A rail 0.70 above its tread
# — a baluster with no rail on it — puts a 0.70 rise beside every tread in this stair and makes
# THAT the number the whole facility reports instead of the great steps' 0.18. The continuous
# rail is what saves it: every column along the balustrade has rail over it, and the LOWEST that
# rail ever reads is 0.835 (see the handrail section for where that number comes from), clear of
# the cutoff. Nothing between a tread and its rail is allowed to top out between 0.19 and 0.75
# above it, which is why the balusters carry no separate plinth of their own and why the raking
# plinth below sits exactly one riser and no more above the tread beside it.
#
# THE RAKING RUNS ARE PLACED BY DOUBLING. `repeat` steps along one axis; a stair balustrade steps
# along two at once, and the balusters must stay upright, so a rotated repeat is no use either.
# One baluster, unioned with itself carried one tread up the rake, gives two; that with itself
# carried two treads gives four, then eight. Twelve is eight and four; eleven is eight, two and
# one. Four lines for twelve balusters, and every one of them is a translate of the same shape.

# AND THEY STAND ON THE NOSING LINE, NOT ON THE TREADS. A rail is a fixed height over the line
# the nosings make; a tread is level and the line is not, so a baluster standing on the middle of
# a tread is 0.09 short of a rail that is straight, and there is a 90 mm slot the length of the
# balustrade. The first version of this file had exactly that, in both flights, and it is invisible
# on a contact sheet because a baluster and its rail are 0.18 apart in a picture. What fixes it is
# the RAKING PLINTH below: a band 0.09 (M/5) deep whose top face IS the nosing line, filling the
# saw-tooth between the line and the treads, so every baluster stands on one plane and the
# doubling's own step of one tread up and one run along is exactly the plinth's own slope.
#
# The plinth top is between 0 and one riser above the tread beside it, which is 0.18 — the
# building's own riser and inside the walkability check's own step. Anything taller there is a
# rise the facility would report instead of the great steps'.
let stair_ra_1  = translate { ionic_baluster } 11.94 1.98 3.24
let stair_ra_2  = union { stair_ra_1  translate { stair_ra_1 } -0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_ra_4  = union { stair_ra_2  translate { stair_ra_2 } -0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_ra_8  = union { stair_ra_4  translate { stair_ra_4 } -1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_ra_12 = union { stair_ra_8  translate { stair_ra_4 } -2.56 1.44 0 }

let stair_bal_a_s = stair_ra_12
let stair_bal_a_n = translate { stair_ra_12 } 0 0 1.17

let stair_rb_1  = translate { ionic_baluster } 8.10 4.32 5.49
let stair_rb_2  = union { stair_rb_1  translate { stair_rb_1 } 0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_rb_4  = union { stair_rb_2  translate { stair_rb_2 } 0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_rb_8  = union { stair_rb_4  translate { stair_rb_4 } 1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_bal_b = union { stair_rb_8  translate { stair_rb_2 } 2.56 1.44 0
                                      translate { stair_rb_1 } 3.20 1.80 0 }

# The level runs: the landing's west and south edges, both ends of the well, and the gallery's
# south and west edges. Same 0.32 pitch as the flights, so the whole balustrade is one rhythm.
let stair_bal_lw = translate { repeat { translate { ionic_baluster } 6.84 4.14 0 } z=0.32 nz=4 } 0 0 4.86
let stair_bal_ls = translate { repeat { translate { ionic_baluster } 0 4.14 3.24 } x=0.32 nx=1 } 7.55 0 0
let stair_bal_lwell = translate { repeat { translate { ionic_baluster } 8.01 4.14 0 } z=0.32 nz=1 } 0 0 4.95
let stair_bal_gs = translate { repeat { translate { ionic_baluster } 0 6.30 4.59 } x=0.32 nx=4 } 13.36 0 0
let stair_bal_gwell = translate { repeat { translate { ionic_baluster } 11.71 6.30 0 } z=0.32 nz=1 } 0 0 4.95

let stair_balusters = union {
    stair_bal_a_s stair_bal_a_n stair_bal_b
    stair_bal_lw stair_bal_ls stair_bal_lwell stair_bal_gs stair_bal_gwell
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE HANDRAIL — a moulded section, 0.18 wide (0.4 M) and 0.225 tall (M/2)
# ================================================================================================
#
#   -0.225 .. -0.180   fillet   M/10 tall, 0.15 wide, the band the balusters die into
#   -0.180 .. -0.090   core     M/5 tall, 0.12 wide, the plain neck
#   -0.090 ..  0.000   ovolo    M/5, a quarter round of 0.09 either side — THE WIDEST MEMBER, and
#                               the only one 0.18 across, so it is what every column finds
#
# measured DOWN from the rail top, because that is the surface a hand takes and the only one that
# has to be a fixed height over a tread. Two ovolos rather than a `mirror`: mirror folds to the
# absolute value of a coordinate, so a mirrored section has to be built at zero and carried, and
# three of these five rails are already being built at zero for the rotation. Two lines is cheaper
# than two frames.
#
# BOTH OF THOSE NUMBERS ARE SET BY THE WALKABILITY CHECK AND NOT BY A HAND. measure.cpp looks down
# EVERY COLUMN of voxels; the column that grazes the very edge of a rail does not find the crown,
# it finds whichever member is widest there. Built 0.18 tall with the fillet the widest, that edge
# column topped out 0.7375 above the landing beside it — under the 0.75 the check treats as a step
# somebody meant to take, so this handrail, and not the great south steps, was the worst rise in
# the entire facility. It reported as "0.75 m" and it took decoding a voxel index to find.
#
# So the rail is M/2 tall and THE OVOLO IS THE WIDEST MEMBER — 0.18 across against the fillet's
# 0.15 and the core's 0.12. Widening the fillet to match was tried first and is not enough: the
# ovolo is a quarter round, so at the very edge of its 0.09 it has no height at all, and the
# outermost sampled column found the fillet under it again at 0.77. With the fillet pulled in,
# the shallowest top any column under this rail can have is the ovolo's own springing,
# 0.925 - 0.09 = 0.835 above the surface, clear of the cutoff by 0.085 — nearly three voxels at
# metre 32. A domed rail always has a thin edge where the crown has run out; what decides whether
# it is a bad step is what is UNDER that edge, and the answer has to be nothing.
#
# THE THREE RAKING RAILS are one straight section built about the origin and turned about z by the
# stair's own slope. The lower flight descends toward +x and the upper one climbs toward +x, so
# they are the SAME section turned opposite ways, which is the check that the angle is right: if
# one of them is parallel to its nosings the other is not.

# Two lengths, both starting at local x = 0, which is the HEAD of the lower flight and the FOOT of
# the upper one. Both ends of every rail die inside a newel: 4.45 along the rake from (8.10, 5.02)
# arrives at (11.98, 2.84) and 4.20 from (8.10, 5.20) at (11.76, 7.26), which are the middles of
# the newels at 12.03 and 11.71. A rail that stops short of its newel leaves a slot; one that runs
# past it leaves a spike over the gallery, which is what the first pass did and what the gallery's
# own head-height probe found.
let stair_rail_a_fil = fillet 0.00 -0.225 -0.075  4.60 -0.18  0.075 run=x
let stair_rail_a_cor = box    0.00 -0.18  -0.06   4.60 -0.09  0.06  round=0.012
let stair_rail_a_ovl = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   4.60  0.00  0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_a_ovr = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   4.60  0.00 -0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_a_sec = union {
    stair_rail_a_fil stair_rail_a_cor stair_rail_a_ovl stair_rail_a_ovr
}

let stair_rail_b_fil = fillet 0.00 -0.225 -0.075  4.20 -0.18  0.075 run=x
let stair_rail_b_cor = box    0.00 -0.18  -0.06   4.20 -0.09  0.06  round=0.012
let stair_rail_b_ovl = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   4.20  0.00  0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_b_ovr = ovolo  0.00 -0.09   0.00   4.20  0.00 -0.1125 run=x
let stair_rail_b_sec = union {
    stair_rail_b_fil stair_rail_b_cor stair_rail_b_ovl stair_rail_b_ovr
}

let stair_rail_down = rotate { stair_rail_a_sec } z=-0.08154932
let stair_rail_up   = rotate { stair_rail_b_sec } z=0.08154932

let stair_rail_a_s = translate { stair_rail_down } 8.10 5.065 3.24
let stair_rail_a_n = translate { stair_rail_down } 8.10 5.065 4.41
let stair_rail_b   = translate { stair_rail_up }   8.10 5.245 5.49

# The raking plinths, in the same two frames but with local y = 0 on the NOSING LINE rather than
# on the rail top, which is the only datum a balustrade on a stair has.
let stair_plin_a_sec = fillet 0.00 -0.09 -0.09   4.60 0.00 0.09 run=x
let stair_plin_b_sec = fillet 0.00 -0.09 -0.09   4.20 0.00 0.09 run=x
let stair_plin_down = rotate { stair_plin_a_sec } z=-0.08154932
let stair_plin_up   = rotate { stair_plin_b_sec } z=0.08154932

let stair_plinths = union {
    translate { stair_plin_down } 8.10 4.14 3.24
    translate { stair_plin_down } 8.10 4.14 4.41
    translate { stair_plin_up }   8.10 4.32 5.49
}

# The level rails. Built where they stand, so the two ovolos are written either side of the run
# rather than folded. `run=x` reads the section in (z, y); the default `run=z` reads it in (x, y).
let stair_rail_lw = union {
    fillet 6.765 4.840 3.15   6.915 4.885 6.60
    box    6.78  4.885 3.15   6.90  4.975 6.60 round=0.012
    ovolo  6.84  4.975 3.15   6.9525 5.065 6.60
    ovolo  6.84  4.975 3.15   6.7275 5.065 6.60
}
let stair_rail_ls = union {
    fillet 6.75 4.840 3.165   8.10 4.885 3.315 run=x
    box    6.75 4.885 3.18    8.10 4.975 3.30  round=0.012
    ovolo  6.75 4.975 3.24    8.10 5.065 3.3525 run=x
    ovolo  6.75 4.975 3.24    8.10 5.065 3.1275 run=x
}
let stair_rail_lwell = union {
    fillet 7.935 4.840 4.41   8.085 4.885 5.49
    box    7.95  4.885 4.41   8.07  4.975 5.49 round=0.012
    ovolo  8.01  4.975 4.41   8.1225 5.065 5.49
    ovolo  8.01  4.975 4.41   7.8975 5.065 5.49
}
let stair_rail_gs = union {
    fillet 11.62 7.000 4.515  15.10 7.045 4.665 run=x
    box    11.62 7.045 4.53   15.10 7.135 4.65  round=0.012
    ovolo  11.62 7.135 4.59   15.10 7.225 4.7025 run=x
    ovolo  11.62 7.135 4.59   15.10 7.225 4.4775 run=x
}
let stair_rail_gwell = union {
    fillet 11.635 7.000 4.59   11.785 7.045 5.49
    box    11.65  7.045 4.59   11.77  7.135 5.49 round=0.012
    ovolo  11.71  7.135 4.59   11.8225 7.225 5.49
    ovolo  11.71  7.135 4.59   11.5975 7.225 5.49
}

let stair_rails = union {
    stair_rail_a_s stair_rail_a_n stair_rail_b
    stair_rail_lw stair_rail_ls stair_rail_lwell stair_rail_gs stair_rail_gwell
}

# THE NEWELS. 0.225 square (M/2), from the surface they stand on to the rail top and NOT ONE
# MILLIMETRE HIGHER — a finial standing 0.18 over a rail is a 0.18 rise beside it, which is the
# building's own riser and would tie the great steps for the worst rise in the facility. Flush is
# free; see the pier note above for the same arithmetic.
# Written 0.012 inside its own faces so the round puts them back, for the reason given over the
# lower flight's treads: a newel 12 mm proud of the rail it terminates is a 7-voxel rise beside a
# building whose worst step is meant to be 6. 0.225 square is exactly the rail's own widest member.
let stair_newel = box -0.1005 -0.018 -0.1005   0.1005 0.913 0.1005 round=0.012

# The two at the foot stand on the FLOOR and not on a tread, and the floor is one riser below the
# first nosing, so they are one riser taller. Both numbers are the rail's: 0.925 over the surface
# the rail is 0.925 over, 1.105 over the one it is 1.105 over. A newel that stops short of its rail
# is a 0.18 rise beside the rail, which is the thing this whole balustrade is arranged to avoid.
let stair_newel_foot = box -0.1005 -0.018 -0.1005   0.1005 1.093 0.1005 round=0.012

# The one at the head of the well, at (8.01, 5.49), is the tall one too, and for the same reason
# in reverse: the raking rail leaves the landing 0.18 higher than the level rail arrives at it,
# because a rail is a fixed height over its own nosing and the first nosing is one riser up. The
# newel is what absorbs that, which is what a newel is for.
let stair_newels = union {
    translate { stair_newel_foot } 12.12 1.80 3.24
    translate { stair_newel_foot } 12.12 1.80 4.41
    translate { stair_newel }  6.84 4.14 3.24
    translate { stair_newel }  6.84 4.14 6.51
    translate { stair_newel }  8.01 4.14 4.41
    translate { stair_newel_foot } 8.01 4.14 5.49
    translate { stair_newel } 11.71 6.30 4.59
    translate { stair_newel } 11.71 6.30 5.49
    translate { stair_newel } 15.01 6.30 4.59
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================

let stair_east = union {
    stair_flight_a stair_landing stair_flight_b stair_gallery
    stair_piers stair_plinths stair_balusters stair_rails stair_newels
}

let part_stair = mirror { stair_east } axis=x

# This part removes nothing. It stands in air the walls and the podium already leave, and it is
# bedded INTO its neighbours rather than cut out of them. Bound anyway, as a point buried in the
# podium's plinth where it can never be seen, because the manifest's `hollowed` difference asks
# for `void_stair` by name and must not have to know whether this fragment needed one.
let void_stair = sphere 0 -0.30 3.30 r=0.02

# ================================================================================================
# THE HEAD-HEIGHT PROBE — the measurement this part exists to make
# ================================================================================================
#
# Not part of the building. `stair_probe_head` is the volume a person's head sweeps through every
# place this stair lets them stand, INTERSECTED with the stair. Run it and it must be empty:
#
#     --clip-part stair_probe_head        -> "the clip is empty"
#
# Six volumes, each 2.10 m over a surface and each pulled 0.03 in from the balustrades so it is
# the walking width and not the handrail it tests:
#
#   pass    the passage under the stair: 1.80 to 3.90 over the whole footprint. This is the one
#           that matters. The lower flight's spandrel and the four piers are solid from the floor
#           and are taken out of it, because you walk round a pier and under a soffit.
#   rake_a  2.10 over the lower flight's nosing line, between its two balustrades
#   rake_b  2.10 over the upper flight's nosing line, between its balustrade and the wall
#   land    2.10 over the half landing
#   gall    2.10 over the gallery
#
# The two raking volumes are rotated boxes in the flights' own frames, which is how a stair is
# checked on paper: a box standing on the nosing line, turned by the pitch.
#
# WHAT THE PROBE CANNOT SEE, so that nobody trusts it further than it goes: it tests THIS part
# against ITSELF. Something another fragment hangs over the passage later will not show up here.

# WHAT COMES OUT OF THE PASSAGE VOLUME, and why it is a plan footprint and not the shapes. You
# walk ROUND a pier and UNDER a soffit, so a pier is not a head-height failure — but nor is the
# handrail two metres up in the air over the lower flight, and that stands inside the passage's
# box just as much as the pier does. So what is subtracted is the GROUND PLAN of everything that
# rises off the floor: the lower flight with its balustrade and its two foot newels, and the four
# piers. A shape would have left the balusters standing in the probe and reported them as low
# ceilings, which is exactly what the first version of this file did.
let stair_probe_box   = box 6.30 1.80 3.15   15.30 3.90 6.75
let stair_probe_pl_a  = box 7.95 1.70 3.10   12.30 4.00 4.55
let stair_probe_pl_p1 = box 6.65 1.70 4.40    7.30 4.00 5.05
let stair_probe_pl_p2 = box 6.65 1.70 5.75    7.30 4.00 6.40
let stair_probe_pl_p3 = box 11.60 1.70 4.40  12.25 4.00 5.05
let stair_probe_pl_p4 = box 11.60 1.70 6.20  12.25 4.00 6.85
let stair_probe_stand = union {
    stair_probe_pl_a stair_probe_pl_p1 stair_probe_pl_p2 stair_probe_pl_p3 stair_probe_pl_p4
}
let stair_probe_pass = difference { stair_probe_box stair_probe_stand }

# THE VOLUME OVER A FLIGHT IS ELEVEN AND TWELVE UPRIGHT BOXES, ONE TO A TREAD, and not one box
# turned by the pitch. A turned box was the first thing tried, because that is how a stair is
# drawn on paper: a rectangle standing on the nosing line. It is the wrong instrument. Head height
# is measured VERTICALLY from the tread you are standing on; the rotated box measures it
# perpendicular to the pitch, which is 13% short, and its lower face lies exactly along the
# nosings, so every nosing in the flight pokes into it and it reports a failure the whole length
# of a stair that has nothing over it at all. One box per tread, from the tread top to 2.10 above
# it, is the thing the rule actually says, and it is placed by the same doubling the balusters are.
let stair_pra_1  = box 11.62 1.98 3.36   11.94 4.08 4.29
let stair_pra_2  = union { stair_pra_1  translate { stair_pra_1 } -0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_pra_4  = union { stair_pra_2  translate { stair_pra_2 } -0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_pra_8  = union { stair_pra_4  translate { stair_pra_4 } -1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_probe_ra = union { stair_pra_8  translate { stair_pra_4 } -2.56 1.44 0 }

let stair_prb_1  = box 8.10 4.32 5.61   8.42 6.42 6.57
let stair_prb_2  = union { stair_prb_1  translate { stair_prb_1 } 0.32 0.18 0 }
let stair_prb_4  = union { stair_prb_2  translate { stair_prb_2 } 0.64 0.36 0 }
let stair_prb_8  = union { stair_prb_4  translate { stair_prb_4 } 1.28 0.72 0 }
let stair_probe_rb = union { stair_prb_8  translate { stair_prb_2 } 2.56 1.44 0
                                          translate { stair_prb_1 } 3.20 1.80 0 }

let stair_probe_land = box 6.96 4.14 3.36    7.89 6.24 6.57
let stair_probe_gall = box 11.86 6.30 4.74   15.07 8.40 6.57

# The five, each on its own so a failure can be found rather than only seen.
let stair_probe_p_pass = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_pass }
let stair_probe_p_ra   = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_ra }
let stair_probe_p_rb   = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_rb }
let stair_probe_p_land = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_land }
let stair_probe_p_gall = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_gall }

let stair_probe_all = union {
    stair_probe_pass stair_probe_ra stair_probe_rb stair_probe_land stair_probe_gall
}
let stair_probe_head = intersection { stair_east stair_probe_all }

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# A grand stair is not one stone. Granite where it is founded and where a foot lands hardest,
# marble for everything the eye follows up — treads, landings, balusters, newels — and BRONZE on
# the handrail, because the contract says metal where the hand touches it and because a brushed
# metal rail 22 m long, raking through a room lit only by bounce, is a fairer question for a path
# tracer than any flat panel of it. Porphyry in the sunk panels of the piers, which is the only
# saturated colour down in the passage.
#
# Every rule keys on one of this file's own shapes, folded to both wings so the west stair is
# painted by the same rules as the east and cannot drift, and every one says below=0.02: the
# manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of grain at the end, and a test at zero misses
# the voxels the displacement made and leaves a rind of limestone over the part that painted it.
#
# NO WEATHERING, and that is a decision. `weather` displaces a surface, and two surfaces in this
# fragment are dimensioned to fifteen millimetres — the landing soffit and the flight waists —
# while a third, the treads, is what the facility's worst-rise number is measured on. A 14 mm
# desert coat would take the head height under the half landing to 2.101 or to 2.129 depending on
# the noise, and neither the probe above nor any render would tell you which. Inside stone stays
# clean.

let stair_granite = mirror { union { stair_flight_a stair_piers } } axis=x
paint granite where=stair_granite below=0.02

let stair_marble = mirror { union { stair_landing stair_flight_b stair_gallery
                                    stair_plinths stair_balusters stair_newels } } axis=x
paint marble where=stair_marble below=0.02

let stair_bronze = mirror { stair_rails } axis=x
paint bronze where=stair_bronze below=0.02

# The panel key is ONE SHAPE PER PIER and it is not a box: it is the pier's own block shrunk by
# 0.06 on every face, caught in the panel's height band. Every surface inside a sunk panel — its
# floor and its four returns — is 0.09 in from the pier's face and so is 0.03 INSIDE that shrunk
# block; a voxel on the pier's own face sits about 0.01 in, which is 0.05 OUTSIDE it, well past
# the rules' own 0.02. So the porphyry stops dead at the arris of the recess. 0.06 and not 0.0225:
# the first pass shrank by half the recess and painted every pier solid red from top to bottom,
# because a voxel centre is up to half a voxel inside the face it belongs to and the 0.02 test
# then reaches the rest of the way. The margin has to be bigger than the voxel, not than the
# tolerance. Four boxes per pier would do the same and would have to be re-derived every time the
# panel moved; `offset` on a box is exact and costs one node.
#
# THE SIGN IS POSITIVE AND THAT IS THE ONE THAT SHRINKS. `Op::Offset` evaluates as
# `eval(child) + by`, so a POSITIVE `by` requires a point to be that much deeper inside before
# it counts — it shrinks — and a NEGATIVE one grows. BRIEF.md's grammar line shows a negative
# value and says "shrinks or grows" without saying which is which. Written `by=-0.06` this rule
# painted a zone 0.06 BIGGER than the pier and every pier in the passage came back solid
# porphyry from plinth to soffit. See clips/facility/requests/stair.md: two other fragments
# appear to have the same sign the wrong way round, and in their case it deletes ornament
# rather than adding it, which is far harder to notice.
let stair_inlay_low = intersection { offset { stair_pier_body } by=0.06
                                     box  6.60 2.19 4.35    7.35 3.51 5.10 }
let stair_inlay_up  = intersection { offset { stair_tpier_body } by=0.06
                                     box 11.55 2.19 4.35   12.30 5.46 5.10 }
let stair_inlays = mirror {
    union { stair_inlay_low  translate { stair_inlay_low } 0 0 1.35
            stair_inlay_up   translate { stair_inlay_up }  0 0 1.80 }
} axis=x
paint porphyry where=stair_inlays below=0.02
